WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.180
Authentication for Web APIs proves the identity of a user, service, workload, or client application.

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.800
It does not by itself approve every operation.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:22.540
Once the API knows who or what is calling, it still has to decide whether that identity may access the requested resource, perform the action, and use the specific data involved.

4
00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:32.900
Use mature identity providers, OAuth or OpenID Connect services, managed authentication platforms, or framework-supported mechanisms where appropriate.

5
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:45.320
Identity systems involve token issuance, validation, expiration, refresh, revocation, consent, MFA, account recovery, service credentials, and audit requirements.

6
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.420
These details are difficult to maintain safely in custom code.

7
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:59.300
Token-based authentication should be reviewed conceptually around issuer, audience, expiration, signature validation, intended use, and trust relationship.

8
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:12.280
An API should know which authority issued the token, whether the token was meant for this API, whether it is still valid, whether it was protected by an expected signing mechanism, and whether its claims fit the authentication design.

9
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:16.460
Those checks are normal validation, not optional decoration.

10
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.140
Token lifetime and logout expectations deserve careful design.

11
00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.400
Shorter lifetimes reduce long-term exposure but may require refresh flows.

12
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.860
Refresh flows create their own storage, revocation, and monitoring needs.

13
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:38.560
Logout for token-based systems can be different from traditional cookie sign-out, especially when multiple clients or services are involved.

14
00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:46.580
Document the expected behavior so product teams, support teams, and users do not assume a guarantee the system does not provide.

15
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:52.380
Avoid custom password storage, custom token formats, and homegrown identity protocols.

16
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.240
If the application owns credentials, use supported mechanisms and organizational policy.

17
00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:06.740
If an external provider owns identity, keep the API focused on validating the provider relationship and enforcing local authorization.

18
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:15.560
Custom schemes often look simple at first and become fragile once lifecycle, rotation, diagnostics, and cross-client behavior are added.

19
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:17.600
Tokens are sensitive.

20
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:25.700
They should not appear in logs, exception pages, traces, analytics events, support screenshots, or ordinary diagnostics.

21
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:34.760
API teams need enough information to troubleshoot identity failures, but not raw bearer tokens, refresh tokens, secrets, or full assertions.

22
00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.180
Treat token handling as both a security and an operational hygiene requirement.
